WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.610
EVREN ERYUREK: Hello everyone.

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:08.730
Welcome to our session
on data governance.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.610
As some of you may remember,
back in November in London

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.500
we had announced
our partnership,

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.050
and today I'm delighted
to be with Jim again

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:19.210
to talk about our
progress to date.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:23.400
Let's take a look at recent
Goldman Sachs reports.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.480
Security breaches, data loss,
legal and compliance issues

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.610
are slowing down many in
their move to public clouds.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.170
Fundamentally they're worried
about loss of control.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.730
When you look at BigQuery it was
designed as a cloud data data

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:42.970
warehouse.

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:45.990
It was designed to address
the needs of data driven

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.840
organization in a
cloud first world

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:54.910
with its reliability,
security, and its governance.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:59.540
So what is data governance
and why it matters?

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.150
Data governance is
a strategic part

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:07.490
of an organization that sets in
place policies and procedures

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:11.960
to maximize utilization and
value of data for decisions

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:15.440
and operations, while
ensuring that equality

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.710
standards, privacy
standards, security controls,

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:22.370
and regulatory compliance.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:26.840
GCP provides robust
tools and technology

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:32.210
to protect and govern throughout
the lifecycle of the data.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.940
We offer built-in
protection at scale

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:38.960
by default. Data is
automatically encrypted while

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.480
in transit and at rest.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:45.110
We supply tools and technology
to efficiently govern

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:46.770
all your data.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:51.860
For example Cloud DLP helps
you discover and classify

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.200
sensitive data assets.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.410
Cloud IAM provides access
control and visibility,

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:02.810
security policies and our
newly G8 Cloud Data catalog

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:06.080
helps you discover and
manage all your data.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.120
We support compliance
requirements

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.160
with all third-party
certifications.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.850
Finally, we believe
that the trust

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:18.110
is created through transparency,
and for that we provide tools

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:23.510
like Access Transparency
which gives you the visibility

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:26.180
and accesses by
either the support

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:30.870
teams or the engineering
teams at all times.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.960
Let's talk about some
of the enhancements

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.660
that we've done
around data classes

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.000
and fine-grained access.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.670
Now with BigQuery's
column level control

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:45.050
you can start thinking about
data as a set of data classes,

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:47.900
some more sensitive than others.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:52.150
Data catalog will control
policies per data class.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:54.260
Audit logs will refer
to these data classes.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:00.990
Let's take a look at a deeper
level and the use case.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:04.880
Use data catalog to define
a taxonomy of policy attacks

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.700
and add access
permissions for users,

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:12.770
groups apply these policy
tags to BigQuery columns.

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:16.530
In this example, you may
have first responders.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.550
They may need to have access
to some of the PII information

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:22.720
like location and
phone and so forth.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:26.720
On the other hand, auditors
would have additional access

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.160
to maybe financial information.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:34.080
Now you'll be able to provide
all these controls very easily.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:40.140
On GCP we provide a platform
that's end-to-end governed.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.870
You can imagine
almost like two zones.

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:46.050
A free zone where not
much of the sensitive data

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.160
is really present.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.580
But a governed zone where a
lot of your governed data needs

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.060
are placed.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.980
Past couple of
years, we have been

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:59.340
focusing on building the best
in class capabilities for you

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.760
to achieve better governed
zone through BigQuery for all

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.640
your enterprise
needs, by establishing

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.980
fine-grained access
like column, table,

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.810
role levels, and whatever.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.530
By integrating DLP to drive
all data classification.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:19.850
By introducing Data Catalog
to discover and tag your data.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:23.990
However, data governance is
a vast space and many of you

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:26.450
have complex hybrid
environments.

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:28.970
As we announced last
November in London

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:31.220
we have partnered with
Collibra, the market

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:33.470
leading governance
solutions provider

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:35.840
to help you with all
your data governance

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.000
needs in this hybrid world.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.610
And I'm delighted
to inform everyone

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:44.150
that Collibra is now GA on GCP.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.910
And we're working
together on joint roadmaps

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:51.250
to further enhance our
offerings to everyone.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:54.730
As we saw before Data
Catalog and BigQuery allows

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:58.760
you to govern data the finer
granularity through policy tax.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:01.370
But governance is
much more than that.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:04.360
We also need to apply,
revoke, modify access

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:07.480
in response to events
across your enterprise.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:09.310
As I mentioned
earlier, we've already

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:12.940
begun integrating Collibra
into GCP services.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:16.000
Our catalogs are
designed to work together

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.520
to help system
integrate achieve data

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:21.040
governance for hybrid
systems, whether it's

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:25.690
your multi-client needs
or cloud and on premise.

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:30.430
With Collibra on GCP you will
have the best of the two worlds

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.560
to achieve all
your data analytics

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:34.300
and data governance needs.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.310
My partner Jim will now
walk you through Collibra

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:40.048
on GCP journey.

00:05:40.048 --> 00:05:41.210
JIM CUSHMAN: That's great.

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:41.918
Thank you, Evren.

00:05:41.918 --> 00:05:44.750
I really appreciate
the introduction.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:47.340
And excited to share
with you guys a use case.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:49.550
Now there's basically
countless ways

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:52.730
that you can identify value
from this combined offering,

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:55.610
but we're going to settle in on
a use case defined by George.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:58.400
He's a business analyst
working on the Google Analytics

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:00.350
platform and he has a goal.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:02.950
His goal is customer
satisfaction.

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:05.450
Now that's a year-long
endeavor, but over the year

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:06.950
he's looking for
different data sets

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:08.690
that answer different
questions that

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:11.810
show how customer satisfaction
is achieved, maintained,

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:13.675
it grows, and it reduces churn.

00:06:13.675 --> 00:06:15.050
So in this situation,
we're going

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:17.270
to focus on a moment
in time where George

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:20.900
is looking for additional data
beyond what George is working

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:25.447
with thus far to answer some
questions about customer churn.

00:06:25.447 --> 00:06:27.530
The process is going to
follow something like this

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:28.870
and it's going to be
a use case run-- what

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:30.350
we call data provisioning.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.767
This is a situation where
there's only a subset of data

00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:36.350
that George currently has access
to, but looking for what else

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:38.190
might be available to use.

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:40.040
So George will start
with an example

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:44.000
of using Collibra to actually
work on the Google Cloud

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.260
to search the catalog.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:48.770
And once that's done our job
is to help navigate George

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:51.440
through the shopping experience,
go out and fetch the data

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.700
for George, and present it
back to George for consumption.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.040
So let's go ahead and jump in.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:58.040
So let's suppose George
starts-- the first thing

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:00.980
that George is going to do
using this shopping metaphor is

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:02.470
try to find a door to open.

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:05.782
And so George being kind
of a non-technical person

00:07:05.782 --> 00:07:07.490
might start with more
of a business term.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:10.390
So George is going to
type in "customer churn".

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:13.610
And customer churn for
George is a definition that's

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:15.580
been defined in his
business glossary

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:18.260
and is shared within
the entire company.

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:20.450
Now when George
first sees this he

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:23.570
can then navigate to
different facts and facets

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:27.200
such as picking business terms,
and these business terms then

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.220
provide various things
within the glossary

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:30.930
that George can choose from.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:32.690
If George chooses
customer churn it

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:35.230
takes George to the next step.

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:37.160
Now this is like
opening up a door

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:41.337
to our graph of information
available to George.

00:07:41.337 --> 00:07:43.170
And some of the things
that George might see

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:45.980
is which models are actually
available underneath

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:47.450
the definition of
customer churn,

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:50.450
such as customer
order or product.

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:53.370
Some financial metrics,
tags, and other things.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:55.010
And this is a means
of George basically

00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:56.750
getting oriented
within the store

00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:59.060
and finding out
where to go to next.

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:01.190
And let's suppose
George says customer

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:03.080
is a really important
thing, but I'd

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:04.772
like to know a little
bit more about it.

00:08:04.772 --> 00:08:06.230
So one of the first
things we do is

00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:10.190
show George what is the content
of customer as a logical model.

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:12.440
And here we're listing all
of the different attributes

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:14.870
and fields that really
make up the definition

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:17.650
of the canonical
definition of a customer.

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:21.617
In any one of these George can
click on one of the definitions

00:08:21.617 --> 00:08:22.700
and learn more about them.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:25.820
Again, as an asset within the
graph that again relates up

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:27.690
to the customer demand model.

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:30.920
But again, back to customer
churn as a business term.

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:32.640
Now, Cliff might
say this is great.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:33.703
I like the content here.

00:08:33.703 --> 00:08:35.120
So let me learn
more, so I'm going

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:37.480
to navigate down the aisle.

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:39.320
As Cliff navigates
down the aisle

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:41.289
of this-- kind of
customer defined aisle--

00:08:41.289 --> 00:08:44.030
we're going to find
various things that

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:45.590
are groups such as systems.

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:49.347
And within systems
might have databases,

00:08:49.347 --> 00:08:50.930
and with them that
might have schemas.

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:52.860
And eventually you
get down to tables.

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:54.560
These are catalog
assets that tell

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:57.740
Cliff about the various
things that the company is

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:00.500
aware of that have been
classified and mapped

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:03.027
to the logical
model of customer.

00:09:03.027 --> 00:09:05.360
And so this kind of gives you
an inventory of ultimately

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:09.680
what is in this file that the
shopping known by customer.

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:12.850
Furthermore, Cliff might
want to say OK, tell me more.

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:17.000
And this is where we show the
integration and the inheritance

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:21.200
to a table all the way back
to policies, and rules,

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:22.910
and regulations,
and limitations,

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:26.300
and reference data that make up
the body of governance that's

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:27.870
within the Collibra system.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:29.330
So in this situation,
Cliff can see

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:32.570
very quickly that I had a table
within here called customer

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:36.290
product sales that had actually
been classified through machine

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:39.740
learning to the customer
domain, and by definition

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:41.360
then it inherits
all the items that

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:44.030
are classifications and
standards and policies

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:46.100
that you see in
green illustrated

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:48.750
to the left of the screen.

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:51.822
Once Cliff understands this,
Cliff might say, OK I like this

00:09:51.822 --> 00:09:54.030
but I want to know where
these products have actually

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:54.660
come from.

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:57.060
Where do these tables come
from and where do they go,

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:58.200
and maybe what happens?

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:00.430
And that's what we call lineage.

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:02.610
This is the idea of how did
the data actually arrive

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:04.260
at this point in destination?

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:06.450
Where else does it go
after this destination?

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:07.590
Who uses it?

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:10.170
And did anything happen to
it on its destination to me,

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:14.240
such as some type of
transformation object?

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:16.740
So we can actually capture all
the underlying transformation

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:20.190
that may have happened
to this physical asset

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:22.530
that you're considering
for shopping purposes.

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:24.710
So this gives Cliff like a
where's this stuff made--

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:25.777
is it made in the US?

00:10:25.777 --> 00:10:26.610
Is it made in Italy?

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:27.720
Somewhere else?

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:31.260
I would like to know, as well
as what happened on its journey

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:32.753
along the way.

00:10:32.753 --> 00:10:34.170
Now the next thing
Cliff might say

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:36.690
is, OK I'm now down to
five different items

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:38.370
to choose on the shelf.

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.200
How do I delineate between them?

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:42.690
How do I know which
one is right for me?

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:46.530
Well that oftentimes is being
done through tribal knowledge

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:49.050
or what did I do in the
past, but it's generally not

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:51.300
objective or defendable
and doesn't always lead

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:53.260
to trustworthy information.

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:56.010
So what we offer in
the Collibra solution

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:58.920
are highly visible
and easy visualization

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:00.760
around a profile score.

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:05.130
And the goal is to provide Cliff
very quickly front of label

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:08.110
demarcation about what is
the best for what they want.

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:11.190
So in this situation we're
showing various attributes that

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:14.320
make a customer of
interest to George,

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:16.910
and showing which system
actually has the best.

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:18.510
So for example the
customer Billings

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:21.070
seems to have the
high score for email,

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:22.680
whereas customer
product sales has

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:25.883
the best information for both
address and for churn date.

00:11:25.883 --> 00:11:27.300
Since those are
important it seems

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:29.580
like customer product sales
is something that would

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:32.400
be pretty important to George.

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:34.427
Now maybe that's not
enough front of label.

00:11:34.427 --> 00:11:36.510
Some of us shoppers like
to turn the label around,

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:39.210
and if necessary we can get
some detailed statistical

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:42.330
information about that
column using stats

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:44.670
around no values,
anonymous values,

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:46.860
distribution
frequencies, et cetera,

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:50.000
that can really help Cliff
choose which of these data

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:54.083
sets to use for his analysis.

00:11:54.083 --> 00:11:56.000
Now Cliff has said OK,
this is the one I want.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.640
I'm adding customer
products sales,

00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:59.630
as you see illustrated
in the center here.

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:01.652
And this is in Cliff's
shopping basket.

00:12:01.652 --> 00:12:04.110
Now you can see there are other
data sets that were already

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:04.810
in there.

00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:07.060
Let's just assume Cliff had
already shopped for things

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:08.922
and maybe these were
sitting in wait.

00:12:08.922 --> 00:12:11.130
But the next big thing that
Cliff wants to do is say,

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:13.588
you know, I'm not just buying
product at the grocery store,

00:12:13.588 --> 00:12:17.440
I'm actually asking for data
and I need to ask for a request.

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:20.130
So Cliff starts the
request process.

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:23.460
And each customer can
set up its own need

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:25.390
for how they manage
this process.

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:27.690
But ultimately the
data owner itself

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:30.750
that would grant
authorization to this data

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:32.730
would want to know
why do you want it?

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:34.620
How long are you
going to use it?

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:36.900
Where are you going
to use it, et cetera.

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:42.890
And so George enters this level
of information in the data set

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:43.562
request.

00:12:43.562 --> 00:12:45.270
And one thing I'll
draw your attention to

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:48.420
is that George has
actually selected lease.

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:50.760
Lease is this notion of
a short term duration

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:53.040
but I want a physical
copy of the data,

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:56.222
whereas borrow would be an
example of something I just

00:12:56.222 --> 00:12:57.930
want to have virtualized
for a very, very

00:12:57.930 --> 00:12:58.700
short period of time.

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:00.367
And buy would be I
actually want to take

00:13:00.367 --> 00:13:02.422
a full copy going forward.

00:13:02.422 --> 00:13:03.880
And that ultimately
might also say,

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:05.120
where do I want to take it?

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:07.440
And here we're actually
going to bring the data set

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:08.850
to Google BigQuery.

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:11.690
And of course there
are dates for how long

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:14.000
George would like to use it.

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:16.630
Now after George has actually
submitted his request

00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:18.327
it needs to go over
to the data owner.

00:13:18.327 --> 00:13:20.160
So we're going to switch
perspectives really

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:24.300
quickly and look at Joanna, and
she receives an inbound request

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:25.480
from George.

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:29.250
And so you can see that Joanna
is seeing the same information

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:32.880
that George entered in in the
request in the center panel,

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:35.460
and off to the right is
where Joanna would then

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:38.520
determine whether she wants to
grant approval for this or not.

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:41.340
Now, I'm showing you a
manual version for this now,

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:43.500
obviously for effect,
but you can set up

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:46.230
rules and workflows that can
automate this process based

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:49.440
on who's making the request,
for what purpose, for how long,

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:51.130
and where is it going to go.

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:53.220
But you can either
manually do it

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:55.290
or you can set up
automated rules.

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:57.720
But since she actually
approves this request then

00:13:57.720 --> 00:13:59.330
it's going to go back to George.

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:01.920
And when it comes back to
George there's one last thing

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:03.100
that he has to do.

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:05.310
He has to acknowledge
all of the items

00:14:05.310 --> 00:14:08.970
to the right that are part
and parcel of actually

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:11.010
taking a lease of data
for a period of time

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:11.880
to Google BigQuery.

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:13.770
And you'll remember
those were established

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:16.950
in those green boxes earlier on
when we looked at the customer

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:17.820
domain.

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:19.380
And they were all
inherited through

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:23.530
to the data set that
George is now asking for.

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:27.870
So once George has actually
approved of this request

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:29.850
he can now check out.

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:32.670
And so this the really
interesting part, because again

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:34.140
George is not a technician.

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:35.640
We don't want
George to have to do

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.020
a bunch of assembly, et cetera.

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:40.310
We're now going to kick off
a data provisioning process.

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:44.020
And so we really are finished
up with step one here.

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:46.590
And then the next step
is that we're actually

00:14:46.590 --> 00:14:48.577
going to have an
edge-based appliance.

00:14:48.577 --> 00:14:50.160
It really is close
to the data center,

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:51.243
wherever it happens to be.

00:14:51.243 --> 00:14:53.490
Suppose it's on
your own premise.

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:55.275
It could be in another cloud.

00:14:55.275 --> 00:14:56.650
It could be a
SaaS-based solution

00:14:56.650 --> 00:14:58.500
that you're working with.

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:01.290
It is this virtual appliance
that actually is reading back

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:04.290
to the Google Cloud server
to talk to the bigger cloud

00:15:04.290 --> 00:15:06.690
and say, do I have
any instructions?

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:08.730
And the instructions
would be this request

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:11.880
that George has asked for
and received approval.

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:13.710
They would be
downloaded and they

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:15.450
would execute a
distributed query

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:18.780
against the underlying
data set or data sets that

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:20.070
have been approved.

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:22.620
After that data is extracted
it would then be composited,

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:24.630
which is basically
the process of looking

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:27.300
for any duplication
within that data set

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:29.790
and compositing it
down to a golden record

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:33.060
choosing the best attributes
from amongst the contributors.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:34.650
Once that's been
done then we also

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:37.380
want to acknowledge that
not all Georges are alike.

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:40.470
Some Georges might have access
to certain data elements

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:42.090
that others are not.

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:44.580
And that's where we deploy
this policy enforcement

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:45.750
around access management.

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:48.180
We would actually eliminate
data that's not actually

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:51.015
available for George to
look at, either by redacting

00:15:51.015 --> 00:15:54.270
it or masking it before it's
passed along to be loaded

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:56.760
into BigQuery for the request.

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:59.040
Once that's done the
data is then encrypted,

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:02.280
transformed up to the
appropriate landing

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:04.800
spot for BigQuery, and
then subsequently loaded

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:05.850
into BigQuery.

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:08.070
And once that's
been done now George

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:11.230
is notified that the data
set is ready for processing.

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:13.467
Now when the data is ready
for processing George

00:16:13.467 --> 00:16:14.550
can do a couple of things.

00:16:14.550 --> 00:16:18.600
He can look at the data in the
raw directly within BigQuery.

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:20.790
But more likely George
is going to want

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:24.060
to look at this and VI reporting
or analytic environment

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:26.120
of choice, and let's
suppose it's Looker.

00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:28.480
And so that's
exactly how George is

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:30.600
going to start working
with this new data set

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:32.960
and maybe either alone or in
combination with other data

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:38.430
sets that were already available
to George within BigQuery

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:44.320
as well as Google Catalog, or
in combination or separately.

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:46.303
So now George has
actually been--

00:16:46.303 --> 00:16:47.970
almost really at the
click of a button--

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:51.480
had data provision for
his use without having

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:52.330
to write any code.

00:16:52.330 --> 00:16:54.560
And it's all been done
under a governed use case.

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:57.140
And so that's really what we
call data use provisioning.

00:16:57.140 --> 00:16:59.310
But to really round
out the use case

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:03.480
we have to send notification and
reminder to George that you're

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:05.910
just using this data for
a short period of time.

00:17:05.910 --> 00:17:07.859
In order to comply with
the data use agreement

00:17:07.859 --> 00:17:10.359
you actually have to get rid
of it when you're done with it.

00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:12.630
So we send a notice
off to George

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:16.358
to let him know exactly
that your use of the data

00:17:16.358 --> 00:17:18.150
has expired and it's
time to get rid of it.

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:20.190
And of course then
we'll notify Joanna

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:21.839
if the same has occurred.

00:17:21.839 --> 00:17:24.635
If in fact, it's been compliant
and George did get rid of it.

00:17:24.635 --> 00:17:26.010
Otherwise, it
would get escalated

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:28.890
and she would be able to follow
up accordingly to make sure

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:31.090
that we stay in compliance.

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:34.440
Now this is just one of many,
many use cases to follow.

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:37.410
We thought it was a
good illustration of how

00:17:37.410 --> 00:17:39.540
the combined solution
works together

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:41.700
in a highly relevant use case.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:44.670
In terms of where
we are today, Evren

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:48.366
was kind enough to announce
that we're available on Google

00:17:48.366 --> 00:17:50.640
Cloud as of July 6th.

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:54.670
Now we are also working on more
and more tighter integration

00:17:54.670 --> 00:17:56.210
and you'll see where we are now.

00:17:56.210 --> 00:17:59.820
We're at storage with
"principle of least privilege"

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:02.660
with data class level securities
available within Google.

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:05.610
And through the
integration with Collibra

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:08.430
we also offer this ability
to shop and discover and make

00:18:08.430 --> 00:18:09.890
that request for data.

00:18:09.890 --> 00:18:12.540
And it could be data that's
already in BigQuery or in cloud

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:14.880
storage that they're
just asking for approval,

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:16.920
or it might actually
need to be provisioned.

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:20.040
In situations where it actually
needs to be provisioned

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:23.350
we'll be covering that within
the next year's roadmap.

00:18:23.350 --> 00:18:25.890
But I think I'd also like to
share with you that later this

00:18:25.890 --> 00:18:27.982
year-- maybe by the
end of the year--

00:18:27.982 --> 00:18:29.940
Collibra Data Intelligence
for your convenience

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:33.870
will be available on
the Google Marketplace.

00:18:33.870 --> 00:18:36.210
And if you've enjoyed what
you've listened to here

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:39.750
I really highly recommend that
you follow your other peers

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:40.920
in the industry.

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:44.520
Even though we've just announced
this a short period of time ago

00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:46.020
we have a large
number of customers

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:47.520
who've already
expressed interest

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:49.650
and are starting
that journey with us.

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:52.830
So if you would like to follow
in their footsteps, please,

00:18:52.830 --> 00:18:56.490
I would suggest you follow
up either at the Google Cloud

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:58.620
website or the Collibra website.

00:18:58.620 --> 00:19:01.260
On behalf of Evren
and myself I would

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:03.630
like to thank you
for joining us,

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:07.310
as well as wish you guys safety
in these difficult times.

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:08.990
Thank you.

